<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Neustria</h1><p class="st-meta">561 CE – 751 CE · capital Soissons</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Western kingdom of the Merovingian Franks, centred on Paris and Soissons, and the long rival of Austrasia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Neustria, the new western land, took shape after the division of 561, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chilperic-i">Chilperic I</a> received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a> and the lands between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a>. It later included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chilperic-i">Chilperic</a> and his queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fredegund">Fredegund</a> waged a bitter feud with Austrasia's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigebert-i">Sigebert I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunhilda-of-austrasia">Brunhild</a>. Their son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlothar-ii">Chlothar II</a> reunited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Frankish kingdoms</a> in 613. Neustria had a strongly Gallo-Roman population and rich abbeys such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilica-of-saint-denis">Saint-Denis</a>, and in the 7th century its mayor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebroin">Ebroin</a> dominated politics.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Austrasian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pippin-of-herstal">Pippin of Herstal</a> defeated the Neustrians at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tertry">Tertry</a> in 687, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a> finally crushed the Neustrian mayor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ragenfrid">Ragenfrid</a> in 717 to 719. After 751 Neustria became a region of the Carolingian realm, and the name was later applied to the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/neustria.jpg" alt="Neustria" width="320" height="317"><figcaption>Image: Kairom13 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francia_714.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>561 CE – 751 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Soissons</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Chilperic I, Fredegund, Chlothar II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin, Frankish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Neustria</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=656&amp;focus=neustria" aria-label="Neustria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4757 456 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Neustria lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5077 712L5077 712M5077 712L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5077 712h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5077 712h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5077 712h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Soissons. <a href="/atlas?year=656&amp;focus=neustria">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
